A||||6||william_thorniii||Blue Reflective Paint?????||||5||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||03-26-2000||05:59 PM||||Friends,<P>I need some advice.  I have recently begun the installation of my blue backlit gages from Super X.  Unfortunately since I am changing the color of the backlighting, it is not just a direct swap.  True the light that shines through the faces will now be the correct color, but there are places (like the odometer) that are front lit.  This light is colored orange by being reflected off of an orange painted piece.  I have every intention of replacing each of my bulbs with blue colored ones, but this still will not rectify this situation.  Therefore I need to paint this piece a blue color to obtain the desired result.  My question is this... do any of you know what type of paint I should seek out for this project?  If so please elaborate on where it can be found.  <P>Thanks again... you guys are the greatest,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||03-26-2000||07:53 PM||||Since tou are replacing the bulbs with blue colored ones, wouldn't white work? Or maybe an aluminized silver or "chrome" spreypaint?  I don't think blue would reflect that well.  But what do I know? Never done anything like that myself, just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||03-26-2000||09:08 PM||||I was wondering: Since the factory color is florescent orange, what about a florescent blue? Maybe check the model car paint and see if something comes close.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||03-26-2000||09:22 PM||||You can try different colors on a test piece out of the car,but you'll probably wind up using a white with just a little blue carefully mixed in.The dark colors will absorb all the light.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-26-2000||10:00 PM||||William, in one of the local yards here, that exact plate is sitting on the floor of one of the Fieros. If you wanted to experiment first, I could get it for you. Wouldn't be any work, its just sitting there, separate from everything else.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||03-26-2000||11:08 PM||||Get the correct shade of blue and mix it with relective grit and brush it on.  Paint stores should have the grit, or can order it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-28-2000||08:44 PM||||Be carefull with metalized paints in this application. Some are electrically conductive. Even if they don't present a dead short the could throw off gauge reading if any wiring comes in contact with the paint.||||reg||1
